<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ulfilas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 311–383</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Wulfila</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ulfilas, or Wulfila, was a Gothic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Cappadocian descent who converted many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> to Arian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. Consecrated around 341, he worked among the Thervingi north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and, after persecution by Athanaric, led his followers into Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a>. He devised the Gothic alphabet and translated the Bible into Gothic, the earliest substantial text in a Germanic language.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ulfilas.jpg" alt="Ulfilas" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fondo Antiguo de la Biblioteca de la Universidad de Sevilla from Sevilla, España · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Germania,_1882_1020017_(4358323359)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
